# 🚀 Terraform with AWS (Deep Dive Example)

Let’s build an AWS EC2 instance using Terraform.

## 📁 Folder Structure

main.tf
variables.tf
outputs.tf

---

# 1️⃣ provider.tf (AWS provider config)

tf
provider "aws" {
  region = "ap-south-1"
}

---

# 2️⃣ variables.tf (input variables)

variable "instance_type" {
  default = "t2.micro"
}

variable "ami" {
  description = "Amazon Linux 2023 AMI"
  default     = "ami-0f58b397bc5c1f2e8"
}

---

# 3️⃣ main.tf (create resources)

### Create EC2 Instance

resource "aws_instance" "my_ec2" {
  ami           = var.ami
  instance_type = var.instance_type
  tags = {
    Name = "Terraform-EC2"
  }
}


---

# 4️⃣ outputs.tf (output EC2 public IP)

tf
output "public_ip" {
  value = aws_instance.my_ec2.public_ip
}


---

# ▶️ Run Terraform Commands

### Initialize


terraform init


### See what changes will happen


terraform plan


### Create AWS EC2 Instance


terraform apply


### Delete everything


terraform destroy


---

# ✨ What Happens Behind the Scenes?

Terraform does the following:

1. Reads your .tf files
2. Communicates with AWS through API
3. Checks if EC2 exists (state file)
4. Creates new EC2
5. Saves details in terraform.tfstate

